rptree --search "TODO"
or:

rptree --search ".py"
You could output something like:
Search results for: TODO

src/rptree/tree.py:42
src/rptree/cli.py:18
tests/test_tree.py:91

## JSON output
This is one I want to add because it makes my program useful to other programs.

rptree --json

Instead of:

src/
├── rptree/
│   ├── cli.py
│   └── tree.py

you could produce:

{
  "name": "src",
  "type": "directory",
  "children": [
    {
      "name": "rptree",
      "type": "directory",
      "children": [
        {
          "name": "cli.py",
          "type": "file"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
Now another Python program could actually consume rptree's output.
Could start creating APIs

## Git awareness
This could make the project particularly interesting for a CS portfolio.

For example:
rptree --git
could show:
Repository-Tree-Graph/
├── src/
│   └── rptree/
│       ├── cli.py
│       └── tree.py
├── tests/
└── README.md

Git:
  Branch: main
  Modified: 2 files
  Untracked: 1 file
  Commits: 37

I could eventually mark files:
├── cli.py       [modified]
├── tree.py
├── README.md    [untracked]
└── tests/

That starts making rptree a repository inspection tool, rather than simply a tree generator.

## Markdown output
For GitHub projects, this could be really nice:
rptree --markdown
producing something suitable for a README:

```text
src/
├── rptree/
│   ├── cli.py
│   └── tree.py
└── tests/
'''

This gives the project an obvious real-world use case.

